<div class="mb-10"><h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Benchmarks</h1>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">Three matched workloads &mdash; a JSON API response, a rendered HTML page, a database read &mdash; through three full stacks on one machine, one load generator, one protocol. Every server returns a <strong class="text-white">byte-identical payload</strong> for the JSON and DB rows, and every stack runs <strong class="text-white">16 workers</strong>.</p>
<div class="rounded-xl border border-amber-500/30 bg-amber-500/5 p-5 mb-8"><p class="text-gray-300 text-sm leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-amber-400">Read this first.</strong> Benchmarks are easy to rig and easy to get wrong. Everything below was measured in one session on a quiet box, after a warm-up pass over all nine endpoints, with the HTTP status of every response verified &mdash; and where Soli loses a cell, the number is printed exactly as measured. The per-operation Soli-vs-Ruby language tables that used to live on this page were retired with it; this page compares <em>frameworks</em>, end to end.</p></div></div>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Setup</h2>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-6"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-medium w-44">Soli</td><td class="py-3 px-4">1.25.0, <code class="text-cyan-400">soli serve .</code>, 16 HTTP workers, SoliDB (loopback HTTP) for the DB row</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-medium w-44">Rails</td><td class="py-3 px-4">8.1.3 + Puma 8.0.2 on Ruby 3.4.9 &mdash; production, eager-loaded, 16 workers &times; 5 threads, PostgreSQL via ActiveRecord</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-medium w-44">Express</td><td class="py-3 px-4">5.2.1 on Node 25.9, 16 cluster workers &mdash; <strong class="text-white">+ EJS 6.0 + Sequelize 6.37.8</strong> (on node-postgres 8.22): Express ships no view layer and no DB layer, both had to be added. The DB rows put it on an <strong class="text-white">ORM</strong>, not the raw driver, so all three stacks compare like for like; the driver number is kept below as a reference</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-medium w-44">Database</td><td class="py-3 px-4">PostgreSQL 18.3 for Rails and Express (same table, same 50 rows); SoliDB for Soli &mdash; all client-server over a local socket, no in-process storage anywhere</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-medium w-44">Load</td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">oha</code> 1.12 &mdash; 30s at concurrency 200 per cell, after warming all nine endpoints</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-medium w-44">Machine</td><td class="py-3 px-4">16-core x86-64 Linux, load generator on the same box</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="text-gray-400 mb-8"><strong class="text-white">CPU/req</strong> is server CPU-time per request, summed across every process of the stack (all 17 for Rails and Express). It is the most portable column here &mdash; unlike req/s it barely moves with core count or client speed.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">JSON &mdash; 50 objects, 2,268 bytes, built in the handler</h2>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-4"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Stack</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">req/s</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">p99</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">CPU/req</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">vs Rails</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Express + EJS + pg</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">122,526</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">5.04 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">102 &micro;s</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">8.2&times;</td></tr>
<tr class="bg-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-bold">Soli</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-white">75,866</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-white">6.15 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-white">180 &micro;s</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-bold">5.1&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Rails + Puma</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">14,954</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">25.77 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">852 &micro;s</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">1.0&times;</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">Soli serialises the API response at 5.1&times; Rails' throughput on 4.7&times; less CPU. Express wins this row outright &mdash; printed as measured. Worth knowing: in Soli, serialising these 50 objects to JSON costs ~180&micro;s where rendering the <em>same data</em> as HTML costs 89&micro;s &mdash; the template engine is currently cheaper than <code class="text-cyan-400">render_json</code>.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Template &mdash; 50-row HTML table + layout, ~3 KB</h2>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-4"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Stack</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">req/s</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">p99</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">CPU/req</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">vs Rails</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
<tr class="bg-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-bold">Soli</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-white font-bold">137,924</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">3.72 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">89 &micro;s</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-bold">11.3&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Express + EJS + pg</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">74,217</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">7.36 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">182 &micro;s</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">6.1&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Rails + Puma</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">12,176</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">32.63 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1,110 &micro;s</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">1.0&times;</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">The strongest row, and the one a server-rendered framework should care about: <strong class="text-white">11.3&times; Rails' throughput on 12.5&times; less CPU</strong> &mdash; and 1.9&times; faster than Express even though Soli's page carries its instant-navigation script (~130 extra bytes of work per request that EJS doesn't do). Soli's ERB engine outrunning a compiled EJS template was not a given.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Database read &mdash; 50 rows, projected columns, 2,268 bytes</h2>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-4"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Stack</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">req/s</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">p99</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">CPU/req</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">vs Rails</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
<tr class="bg-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-bold">Soli</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-white font-bold">36,944</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">6.78 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">215 &micro;s&nbsp;<span class="text-gray-500">(332 incl. SoliDB)</span></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-bold">4.1&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Express + EJS + Sequelize</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">27,761</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">14.35 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">407 &micro;s</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">3.1&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Rails + Puma</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">9,026</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">38.74 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1,352 &micro;s</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">1.0&times;</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="rounded-xl border border-amber-500/30 bg-amber-500/5 p-5 mb-4"><p class="text-gray-300 text-sm leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-amber-400">This row compares database access architectures as much as frameworks.</strong> Each request from Soli is one blocking HTTP round trip to SoliDB per worker &mdash; 16 in flight, no more. Rails holds 80 threads against PostgreSQL; Express's driver is fully asynchronous, effectively unbounded &mdash; which is most of why it still leads here.</p><p class="text-gray-300 text-sm leading-relaxed mt-3"><strong class="text-amber-400">All three stacks go through an ORM in this table.</strong> An earlier revision measured Express on the raw <code class="text-cyan-400">pg</code> driver, which is not the same workload: hand-written SQL with no model layer against two frameworks paying one. Putting Sequelize in the path costs Express <strong class="text-white">34%</strong> &mdash; 42,818 req/s on the driver against 28,388 through the ORM &mdash; and that is the number published, because Soli's and Rails' rows include their ORMs too. If your Node app talks to the database through a driver rather than an ORM, the driver row is the one that describes you. Soli's CPU column shows both truths: 215&micro;s in the Soli process, <strong class="text-white">332&micro;s system-wide once SoliDB's own CPU is counted</strong> &mdash; publishing the smaller number alone would hide an entire process. Even counted that way Soli's DB row costs <strong class="text-white">4.1&times; less system CPU than Rails'</strong>, and the 16-in-flight cap is not the ceiling it looked like: it is enough to lead this row.</p></div>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">Every stack serves the same self-describing hash rows on its fastest idiom for that shape: Soli's <code class="text-cyan-400">Post.pluck(:id, :title, :views).all</code> builds the hashes <strong class="text-white">in the database</strong> (<code class="text-cyan-400">RETURN {id: doc.id, ...}</code>); Rails' is <code class="text-cyan-400">pluck</code> + a <code class="text-cyan-400">map</code> &mdash; the canonical-looking <code class="text-cyan-400">render json: Post.select(:id, :title, :views)</code> measured <strong class="text-white">3.2&times; slower</strong> (3,481 req/s), because it instantiates fifty ActiveRecord models per request; node-postgres builds the objects in the driver. Two lessons worth taking home: projection must happen in the database (Soli fetching full ~15&nbsp;KB documents and projecting client-side measured 13,251 req/s), and if you can accept positional arrays instead of hashes, everyone gets faster &mdash; Soli measured 26,666 and Express 44,515 on the array form of the same route.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Database read + HTML render &mdash; 50 rows from the database into a page, ~3 KB</h2>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">The row a server-rendered framework actually lives on: query, then render. It is the <code class="text-cyan-400">/db</code> read and the <code class="text-cyan-400">/template</code> render in one request, so the response is the same page as the Template row above, byte-for-byte the same size, and the database is the only added variable.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-4"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Stack</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">req/s</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">p99</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">CPU/req</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">vs Rails</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
<tr class="bg-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-bold">Soli</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-white font-bold">39,130</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">6.55 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">188 &micro;s&nbsp;<span class="text-gray-500">(310 incl. SoliDB)</span></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-bold">5.0&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Express + EJS + Sequelize</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">23,626</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">15.09 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">500 &micro;s</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">3.0&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Rails + Puma</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">7,776</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">43.81 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1,590 &micro;s</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">1.0&times;</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">Soli takes this row &mdash; <strong class="text-white">5.0&times; Rails' throughput and 1.7&times; Express's</strong> &mdash; and it is the row whose shape matters most, because it is the only one where every stack does the two things a page does, each through its own ORM. Express on the raw driver reaches 32,416 here, still short of Soli with an ORM in the way.</p>
<div class="rounded-xl border border-emerald-500/30 bg-emerald-500/5 p-5 mb-8"><p class="text-gray-300 text-sm leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-emerald-400">The result worth pausing on is Soli's own:</strong> this row and the JSON row above are within 6% of each other (39,130 and 36,944) on the same query and the same 50 rows. Once a database round trip is in the request it dominates &mdash; whether the result leaves as JSON or as a rendered page is close to noise. The large render-path gap the JSON and Template rows show on in-memory data does not survive contact with a real query, which is worth knowing before optimising a template for a page that is really waiting on the database.</p></div>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Writes &mdash; create, update and delete, one row per request</h2>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">The read rows above are only half of CRUD. These three measure a single write per request against an isolated 800,000-row table, reset to exactly that state before every cell so no stack inherits a table another stack grew or emptied. Update and delete address one row by primary key, drawn at random from the same 1..800,000 range in all three stacks.</p>
<div class="rounded-xl border border-amber-500/30 bg-amber-500/5 p-5 mb-6"><p class="text-gray-300 text-sm leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-amber-400">Durability is matched, and that matters more than anything else here.</strong> SoliDB's writes go through RocksDB's default write path &mdash; the WAL reaches the operating system but is not <code class="text-cyan-400">fsync</code>ed before the write returns. PostgreSQL's default (<code class="text-cyan-400">synchronous_commit=on</code>) <em>does</em> flush before commit returns, which is a stronger guarantee and a much slower one. Compared head to head that way, Soli would be winning an argument the other stacks were not having. So PostgreSQL runs with <code class="text-cyan-400">synchronous_commit=off</code> for these rows, the setting that matches what SoliDB actually promises: survive a process crash, not a power cut. Neither column is "durable writes" &mdash; read them as buffered writes on both sides.</p></div>

<h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-3">Create &mdash; one INSERT per request</h3>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-6"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Stack</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">req/s</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">p99</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">CPU/req</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">vs Rails</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
<tr class="bg-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-bold">Soli</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-white font-bold">35,392</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">8.05 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">121 &micro;s&nbsp;<span class="text-gray-500">(316 incl. SoliDB)</span></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-bold">3.8&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Express + EJS + Sequelize</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">24,482</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">19.59 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">453 &micro;s</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">2.6&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Rails + Puma</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">9,242</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">41.22 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1,336 &micro;s</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">1.0&times;</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-3">Update &mdash; one row by primary key</h3>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-6"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Stack</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">req/s</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">p99</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">CPU/req</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">vs Rails</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
<tr class="bg-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-bold">Soli</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-white font-bold">32,659</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">8.74 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">127 &micro;s&nbsp;<span class="text-gray-500">(352 incl. SoliDB)</span></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-bold">3.0&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Express + EJS + Sequelize</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">22,005</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">18.45 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">500 &micro;s</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">2.0&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Rails + Puma</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">10,748</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">33.35 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1,097 &micro;s</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">1.0&times;</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-3">Delete &mdash; one row by primary key</h3>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-4"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Stack</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">req/s</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">p99</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">CPU/req</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">rows removed</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">vs Rails</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Express + EJS + Sequelize</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">32,339</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">12.85 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">318 &micro;s</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">58% of requests</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">3.0&times;</td></tr>
<tr class="bg-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-bold">Soli</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-white font-bold">29,959</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">8.86 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">147 &micro;s&nbsp;<span class="text-gray-500">(399 incl. SoliDB)</span></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">60% of requests</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-bold">2.7&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Rails + Puma</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">10,953</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">32.09 ms</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1,082 &micro;s</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">82% of requests</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">1.0&times;</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="rounded-xl border border-amber-500/30 bg-amber-500/5 p-5 mb-6"><p class="text-gray-300 text-sm leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-amber-400">Read the delete row with its caveat.</strong> Delete is the one operation that consumes its own workload: a key already deleted is a miss, and a miss is cheaper than a delete. The "rows removed" column is measured, not assumed &mdash; the table is counted before and after each cell &mdash; and it shows the effect plainly: the faster a stack runs, the more of the 800,000-row pool it exhausts and the higher its miss rate climbs. Rails, at a third of the throughput, does the largest share of real deletes. So this row understates the gap to Rails and should be read as a rough ordering, not a precise multiple. The create and update rows have no such problem: every create inserts, and every update targets a key that still exists.</p></div>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">Two things stand out across all three. Soli's own CPU per write is <strong class="text-white">8 to 11&times; lower than Rails'</strong> and roughly a third of Express's, but the system-wide figure &mdash; the number in parentheses, which counts SoliDB's process too &mdash; is where the honest comparison sits, and even there Soli leads. And every stack writes far more slowly than it reads: Soli's create row is 35,392 against 137,924 on the in-memory template row, because a write has to reach another process and a log, whichever framework issued it.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Memory</h2>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-4"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Stack</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Processes</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Idle</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Under load</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
<tr class="bg-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4 text-white font-bold">Soli</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1 &times; 16 threads</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">48 MB</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-emerald-400 font-semibold">72 MB</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Rails + Puma</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">17 (fork + CoW)</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">195 MB</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">918 MB</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Express + EJS + pg</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">17 (fork + CoW)</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">342 MB</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">908 MB</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">Figures are <strong class="text-white">PSS</strong> (proportional set size) summed over the whole process group &mdash; the honest measure for multi-process servers, because summing RSS counts every <code class="text-cyan-400">fork</code>-shared page 17 times (an idle Rails' RSS sum reads 1.9&nbsp;GB against a real 195&nbsp;MB). "Under load" is read at the end of a 30s run on the DB + HTML route. The architectural cause is simple: Soli is one process whose 16 workers are threads; Rails and Express each fork 16 processes with a heap apiece. <strong class="text-white">At idle Soli runs in a quarter of Rails' memory; under load, in a thirteenth.</strong> Soli's own figures grew this release (28 &rarr; 48&nbsp;MB idle): each worker now drives DB I/O on its own reactor with its own connection pool, which is what bought the throughput above.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">The code being measured</h2>
<p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Both apps are idiomatic and the same size &mdash; the DB action in each:</p>
<div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-4">
<div><p class="text-gray-500 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide mb-2">Soli</p>
<pre class="bg-black/40 border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4 overflow-x-auto"><code class="language-soli">class PostsController &lt; Controller
  def db_json
    render_json(Post.pluck(:id, :title, :views).all)
  end
end</code></pre></div>
<div><p class="text-gray-500 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide mb-2">Rails</p>
<pre class="bg-black/40 border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4 overflow-x-auto"><code class="language-ruby">class PostsController &lt; ApplicationController
  def db_json
    render json: Post.pluck(:id, :title, :views)
      .map { |id, title, views| { id: id, title: title, views: views } }
  end
end</code></pre></div>
</div>
<div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-4">
<div><p class="text-gray-500 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide mb-2">Soli &mdash; model, complete</p>
<pre class="bg-black/40 border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4 overflow-x-auto"><code class="language-soli">class Post &lt; Model
end</code></pre></div>
<div><p class="text-gray-500 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide mb-2">Rails &mdash; model + migration</p>
<pre class="bg-black/40 border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4 overflow-x-auto"><code class="language-ruby">class Post &lt; ApplicationRecord
end

# db/migrate/..._create_posts.rb
class CreatePosts &lt; ActiveRecord::Migration[8.1]
  def change
    create_table :posts do |t|
      t.string  :title
      t.integer :views
    end
  end
end</code></pre></div>
</div>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">The controllers, views and routes are line-for-line equivalent; the visible difference is the migration. That is a trade, not a win: SoliDB is schemaless, so Soli needs no migration <em>and</em> gets no database-enforced schema &mdash; Rails guarantees the table's shape, Soli lets you persist anything. The Express version is a working app too, but its view engine, ORM, pooling and template compilation are hand-assembled &mdash; the ~40 lines it takes are the frameworkless tax the other two don't pay.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">What these multiples do and don't mean</h2>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">Trivial handlers measure <em>fixed framework overhead</em>, which is precisely where Rails is weakest &mdash; that is why the JSON and template rows show 5&ndash;11&times; and the DB rows show 4&ndash;5&times;. On a page dominated by real query work the multiple compresses toward the DB row, not the template row. The honest claim this page supports is: <strong class="text-white">Soli's framework overhead is roughly a tenth of Rails' and its render path beats Express's, while database-bound routes are architecture-limited for everyone.</strong> Quote it that way.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Reproducing</h2>
<p class="text-gray-400 mb-3">Warm up first, then measure for a fixed duration &mdash; never a fixed request count:</p>
<pre class="bg-black/40 border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4 overflow-x-auto mb-4"><code class="language-bash">oha -z 8s  -c 100 --no-tui --output-format quiet http://localhost:PORT/route   # warm-up
oha -z 30s -c 200 --no-tui --output-format json  http://localhost:PORT/route</code></pre>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">Three checks before believing any result, including this one. Verify the <strong class="text-white">status codes and payload bytes</strong> &mdash; load generators report a wall of 301s or 500s as 100% "success", and two stacks returning different payloads aren't running the same benchmark. Run <strong class="text-white">30 seconds or more</strong> &mdash; a fixed-count run against a fast server finishes before reaching steady state. And watch the <strong class="text-white">load generator's own CPU</strong> &mdash; co-located, it takes cores from the server, so every absolute number on this page is a floor. When in doubt, trust the CPU/req column: it survives all three mistakes.</p>
